\title{A chain of more than 64 associated type defaults is rejected with a bound-overflow message}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-15}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{language}
\tag{compiler}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-15}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{\strong{Symptom.} A trait declares \code{\verb~|type A1 = Self::A0;~}, \code{\verb~|type A2 = Self::A1;~} and so on. Up to 64 links everything works. With more, the trait and its impls are still accepted, and the first use of the last type reports \code{\verb~|overflow evaluating the bound `S: Tr` (depth limit 64)~} at the use.}
\p{\strong{Cause.} Each default that names another one costs one level of the depth limit that guards trait selection, \code{\verb~|SELECT_DEPTH_LIMIT~}. The chain is not recursive, and the bound named in the message holds.}
\p{\strong{Direction.} Either resolve a chain of defaults without spending selection depth, or report the chain at the trait and name it. Found by the fuzzing of associated types recorded in [ses-TKVW](ses-TKVW).}
